{"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"draft_support": {"enum": ["supported", "contradicted", "insufficient"], "title": "Draft Support", "type": "string"}, "public_support": {"enum": ["supported", "contradicted", "insufficient"], "title": "Public Support", "type": "string"}, "quote_ids": {"items": {"type": "string"}, "maxItems": 3, "title": "Quote Ids", "type": "array"}, "reason": {"maxLength": 240, "title": "Reason", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["draft_support", "public_support", "quote_ids", "reason"], "title": "SelectedAssessment", "type": "object"}

{"question": "Trong điều trị nội nha răng trước hàm trên và hàm dưới, làm thế nào để loại bỏ sự cản trở đường đi của trâm và đạt được đường vào thẳng tới vùng chóp?", "claim": "Việc loại bỏ 'gờ lưỡi' (lingual shoulder) ở vùng cổ răng trong các răng trước hàm trên và hàm dưới là cần thiết để loại bỏ sự uốn cong trâm và đạt được đường vào thẳng tới vùng chóp.", "internal_evidence": [{"evidence_id": "I1", "content": "Khi cần đánh giá nhanh tình trạng khô miệng của người bệnh tại phòng khám, bác sĩ có thể sử dụng một cây đè lưỡi đặt nhẹ nhàng vào niêm mạc má trong khoảng thời gian 10 giây. Nếu dụng cụ này càng dính chặt và khó rơi ra khỏi bề mặt niêm mạc, điều đó chứng tỏ mức độ suy giảm tiết nước bọt và tình trạng khô miệng của bệnh nhân càng trở nên nghiêm trọng."}], "public_quote_options": [{"quote_id": "P1.Q1", "evidence_id": "P1", "text": "For mandibular impacted third molars, the surrounding bone is denser, with thicker cortical plates and the presence of internal and external oblique ridges. Therefore, inferior alveolar, buccal, and lingual nerve blocks are the first-line anesthetic techniques. Alternatively, multiple-site infiltration with a sufficient volume of articaine can also achieve satisfactory anesthesia.30"}, {"quote_id": "P2.Q1", "evidence_id": "P2", "text": "During mandibular third molar extraction, if a tooth or root “disappears” from the socket, CBCT should be performed to determine its position. If the entire tooth has shifted into the lingual space, it can often be palpated through the lingual mucosa. In an attempt to push the tooth upward, partial removal of the lingual bone plate may be necessary to extrude the tooth. If this attempt is unsuccessful or for displaced roots, a triangular incision can be made distal to the mesiolingual aspect of the second molar to reflect the lingual mucoperiosteal flap. Direct visualization allows the retrieval of the displaced tooth/root from the soft tissue space corresponding to the lingual bone plate defect using vascular forceps. Intraoral endoscopy increases safety and efficiency. The lingual nerve should be protected, and hemostasis should be ensured."}, {"quote_id": "P3.Q1", "evidence_id": "P3", "text": "In maxillary and mandibular anterior teeth, where access is typically gained through the lingual or palatal surface, the “lingual shoulder” at the cervical region often restricts the path of the file. Removing this lingual shoulder is essential to eliminate file deflection and achieve true straight-line access to the apical region (Figure 6)."}, {"quote_id": "P4.Q1", "evidence_id": "P4", "text": "The clinical presentation of dentin hypersensitivity is not evenly distributed throughout the mouth. Canines and first premolars tend to be the most frequently affected teeth, followed by incisors, second premolars, and molars, while buccal cervical surfaces are most often involved, presumably because they are more exposed to mechanical trauma from toothbrushing and to dietary acids.[8] Labial, occlusal, distal, and lingual surfaces are also affected, but to a lesser extent, and incisal and palatal surfaces are usually least involved.[9] A greater prevalence on the left side of the dentition has also been observed, possibly reflecting brushing patterns in right-handed individuals.[5]"}], "public_sources": [{"evidence_id": "P1", "title": "Expert consensus on the management of third molar health", "heading_path": ["Body", "Anesthetic choices for impacted third molar surgery"]}, {"evidence_id": "P2", "title": "Expert consensus on the management of third molar health", "heading_path": ["Body", "Management of common severe complications of impacted third molar extraction", "Management of root displacement into the lingual space87"]}, {"evidence_id": "P3", "title": "Clinical Significance and Anatomical Considerations of Apical Patency in Endodontic Therapy: A Comprehensive Review", "heading_path": ["Body", "4. Strategies for Achieving Patency", "4.2. Access Cavity Preparation, Orifice Enlargement, and Straight-Line Access"]}, {"evidence_id": "P4", "title": "Dentin hypersensitivity, an enigma revisited: Mechanisms, diagnosis and therapeutic advances", "heading_path": ["Body", "EPIDEMIOLOGY"]}]}
