{"question": "Trong nghiên cứu về viêm tấy sàn miệng Ludwig ở Somalia, nhóm tuổi nào chiếm tỷ lệ mắc cao nhất trong số các bệnh nhân được khảo sát?", "claim": "Nhóm tuổi từ 20 đến 39 tuổi chiếm tỷ lệ cao nhất với 58,9% tổng số bệnh nhân mắc viêm tấy sàn miệng Ludwig được nghiên cứu tại trung tâm.", "internal_evidence": [{"evidence_id": "I1", "content": "Dữ liệu dịch tễ học lâm sàng từ nghiên cứu ghi nhận nhóm tuổi 20-39 chiếm 53 trường hợp, tương đương 58,9% trong tổng số 90 bệnh nhân."}], "public_quote_options": [{"quote_id": "P1.Q1", "evidence_id": "P1", "text": "In addition, 21 (40.4%) articles demonstrated a low risk of bias [20,32,34,38,40,42–45,49–52,57, 59,62,63,72,75,76,81,83–89,94,96], 30 (57.7%) articles had moderate-risk of bias category [33,35–37,39,41,46–48,53–56,58,60,61,64–71,73,74,77–80] and a study had high risk of bias [31,95]."}, {"quote_id": "P2.Q1", "evidence_id": "P2", "text": "The studies were generally categorized into three quality levels: low, moderate, and high risk of bias. Most studies 40.4% (21/52) had low risk of bias [20,32,34,38,40,42–45,49–52,57,59,62,63,72,75,76,81] while 57.7% (30/52) had a moderate risk of bias [35–39,41,46–48,53–56,58,60,61,64–71,73,74,77–80]. Studies with low risk of bias generally met key criteria like adequate sample size, detailed descriptions of the population and setting, appropriate sampling techniques, and valid and reliable methods for measuring the condition of interest. On the other hand, a study had a high risk of bias [31] due to weaknesses in sample size adequacy and the use of less reliable condition identification methods. (See S3 File)"}, {"quote_id": "P3.Q1", "evidence_id": "P3", "text": "The overall prevalence of hyposalivation was estimated to be 33.0% (95% CI: 25.7%, 40.7%; I2 = 98.5%). The reported prevalence of hyposalivation across the included studies ranged from 6.42% to 70%. The pooled prevalence of hyposalivation based on unstimulated saliva was 39.0% (95% CI: 27.9–50.7%; I² = 98.4%), while the pooled prevalence based on stimulated saliva was 29.9% (95% CI: 20.9–39.7%; I² = 98.6%). Subgroup analyses of hyposalivation prevalence in older adults are shown in the forest plots (Fig. 3a and b) with detailed results provided in Table S7. In subgroup analysis, the pooled prevalence of each group did not differ statistically significantly (p > 0.05) when stratifying data by study designs (p = 0.99), study settings (p = 0.20), age cut-off point (p = 0.17), sex (p = 0.14), methods to define hyposalivation (p = 0.29), and risk of bias (p = 0.77) (Table S7)."}, {"quote_id": "P4.Q1", "evidence_id": "P4", "text": "Table 1
Demographic, clinical, and treatment characteristics of patients with Ludwig’s angina (N = 90)
Characteristic | Category | n (%)
| 20–39 | 53 (58.9)"}], "public_sources": [{"evidence_id": "P1", "title": "Prevalence of dental caries in the primary, mixed and permanent dentitions in Nigeria: A systematic review and meta-analysis", "heading_path": ["Body", "Results", "Characteristics of included studies"]}, {"evidence_id": "P2", "title": "Prevalence of dental caries in the primary, mixed and permanent dentitions in Nigeria: A systematic review and meta-analysis", "heading_path": ["Body", "Results", "Quality assessment"]}, {"evidence_id": "P3", "title": "Prevalence of xerostomia and hyposalivation in older adults: a systematic review and meta-analysis", "heading_path": ["Body", "Results", "Prevalence of hyposalivation and subgroup analysis"]}, {"evidence_id": "P4", "title": "Ludwig’s angina in Somalia: clinical characteristics, emergency management, and outcomes from a tertiary academic referral center", "heading_path": ["Body", "Results", "Patient characteristics"]}]}
