{"question": "Các yếu tố tâm lý và chất lượng cuộc sống liên quan đến sức khỏe răng miệng (OHRQoL) có mối tương tác như thế nào với bốn phân nhóm bệnh niêm mạc miệng cốt lõi?", "claim": "Nhiều yếu tố đóng vai trò trung gian trong mối tương tác hai chiều giữa các yếu tố tâm lý, OHRQoL và bốn phân nhóm bệnh niêm mạc miệng cốt lõi gồm RAU, OLP, OLK và OSF.", "internal_evidence": [{"evidence_id": "I1", "content": "Nhiều yếu tố sinh lý và tâm lý đóng vai trò trung gian trong mối tương tác hai chiều phức tạp giữa chất lượng cuộc sống liên quan đến sức khỏe răng miệng và bốn phân nhóm bệnh niêm mạc miệng cốt lõi gồm viêm loét áp-tơ tái phát, lichen phẳng miệng, bạch sản và xơ hóa niêm mạc miệng."}], "public_quote_options": [{"quote_id": "P1.Q1", "evidence_id": "P1", "text": "OSF is accompanied by anxiety. It imposes a unique dual psychological burden tied to its etiology: first, anxiety from progressive functional impairment, as advancing fibrosis tightens the mucosa and restricts jaw mobility, triggering despair over impending eating/speaking dysfunction—distinct from OLK-related fear of malignant transformation or RAU-associated recurrence anxiety [56]; second, guilt from entrenched betel quid addiction (more severe than in OLK), driven by conflicting awareness of its carcinogenicity amid failed cessation attempts, and compounded by social stigma from oral rigidity and pallor [54]. OSF correlates with anxiety, depression, and areca nut dependence, with sociodemographic factors modulating these psychological burdens; notably, addiction severity and functional anxiety independently predict OHRQoL deficits post-adjustment for fibrosis and pain [38, 51, 74,"}, {"quote_id": "P1.Q2", "evidence_id": "P1", "text": "75], and research on OSF-OHRQoL links remains nascent [39]."}, {"quote_id": "P2.Q1", "evidence_id": "P2", "text": "Numerous factors mediate the bidirectional interaction between psychological factors, OHRQoL, and four core oral mucosal disease (OMDs) subtypes—RAU, OLP, OLK, and OSF. Essential strategies have been identified to address these challenges, including subtype-tailored psychological interventions and stratified OHRQoL tool selection based on instrument psychometrics and clinical needs."}, {"quote_id": "P3.Q1", "evidence_id": "P3", "text": "Subtype-specific mechanistic pathways linking psychological factors to oral mucosal disease (OMDs) pathogenesis are stratified by evidentiary strength—strong (supported by randomized controlled trials or large cohort studies), moderate (well-designed observational investigations), and weak (preliminary or exploratory data)—with established pathways (e.g., T-cell-mediated autoimmunity in OLP) clearly distinguished from emerging mechanistic insights (e.g., TGF-β/Smad-driven fibrosis in OSF). This evidence-based stratification directly informs tailored clinical practice for the four core OMDs subtypes, with mechanistically aligned interventions and OHRQoL assessment strategies to address subtype-unique pathophysiological and psychosocial burdens. For RAU, targeted anxiety screening and cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) are recommended to modulate hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis"}, {"quote_id": "P3.Q2", "evidence_id": "P3", "text": "hyperactivation, thereby reducing mucosal inflammatory activity and ulcer recurrence [9, 34, 41]. For OLP, combined immunomodulatory therapy and stress management interventions serve to rectify Th1/Th2/Th17 immune imbalance, alleviating erosive mucosal lesions and mitigating disease-related psychological distress [13, 25, 49]. OHRQoL assessment tools are similarly stratified by disease course and"}, {"quote_id": "P4.Q1", "evidence_id": "P4", "text": "A structured narrative synthesis was undertaken to synthesize evidence on the psychological factors modulating OHRQoL in four core OMD subtypes (RAU, OLP, OLK, OSF). This synthesis focused on two key themes: The clinical applicability of generic and disease-specific OHRQoL assessment tools for each subtype, and the psychometric performance of these tools in capturing subtype-unique psychological burdens and distress. Additionally, mechanistic links between psychological factors and OMD pathogenesis, and their implications for OHRQoL, were integrated into the synthesis to inform clinical translation."}], "public_sources": [{"evidence_id": "P1", "title": "Psychological factors and quality of life in oral mucosal diseases: A systematic review of assessment tools and clinical implications", "heading_path": ["Body", "Results", "Current research status on psychological factors and OHRQoL in OMDs", "Association between psychological factors and OMDs"]}, {"evidence_id": "P2", "title": "Psychological factors and quality of life in oral mucosal diseases: A systematic review of assessment tools and clinical implications", "heading_path": ["Body", "Conclusion"]}, {"evidence_id": "P3", "title": "Psychological factors and quality of life in oral mucosal diseases: A systematic review of assessment tools and clinical implications", "heading_path": ["Body", "Discussion"]}, {"evidence_id": "P4", "title": "Psychological factors and quality of life in oral mucosal diseases: A systematic review of assessment tools and clinical implications", "heading_path": ["Body", "Methods", "Data synthesis"]}]}
